## 1. Core Responsibilities: The Model Development Lifecycle

The MLE owns the **end-to-end ML lifecycle** — from problem framing through production monitoring. This is a key differentiator: unlike some organizations where production deployment is handed off to backend teams, the MLE at organizations like Atlassian maintains **full-cycle ownership**. [Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer (MLE)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE)

###1.1 Problem Framing & Data Exploration

- **Business problem quantification**: Translating business requirements into measurable ML objectives (e.g., "improve content recommendations" → "increase NDCG@10 by 5% with latency <50ms p99")
- **Feasibility analysis**: Determining if a problem is ML-solvable given data availability, compute constraints, and latency requirements
- **Data discovery**: Exploring data lakes (Socrates), browsing feature store catalogs (Tecton/MLFS), accessing UGC data via privacy-compliant environments with ACLs [Current ML End-to-End Experiment Landscape FY26 H2](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020e8e542e6fbb547dea43317e50413b60f/pages/6676884085/Current+ML+End-to-End+Experiment+Landscape+FY26+H2)

### 1.2 Feature Engineering

- Defining and computing features (activity counts, interaction patterns, temporal aggregations)
- Registering features in the feature store using DSL-based definitions
- Managing online/offline feature serving with training-serving skew prevention
- Point-in-time correctness for historical training data generation [MLFS & Tecton](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TCH1/pages/5576660473/MLFS+Tecton)

### 1.3 Model Training & Architecture Selection

- Selecting model architectures (classical ML → deep learning → LLMs) based on problem characteristics
- Writing training loops with framework-specific APIs (PyTorch DDP, Ray Train, scikit-learn)
- Configuring distributed training across multi-GPU/multi-node setups
- Hyperparameter tuning and tracking experiments via MLflow
- Training on Databricks clusters or ML Fabric (Kubernetes) compute [ML Fabrics Training Infrastructure - Code Analysis](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63d0e8ba8dd199a03e115452/pages/6604046821/ML+Fabrics+Training+Infrastructure+-+Code+Analysis)

### 1.4 Evaluation & Iteration

- Running offline evaluations with task-specific metrics (accuracy, MRR, NDCG, precision/recall, AUC-ROC,sMAPE)
- LLM-as-a-judge evaluations for generative AI outputs
- Golden dataset validation studies
- Regression testing against prompt/model updates
- A/B testing different model variations with statistical rigor [SPIKE: ML Evaluation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202036af61f960604897b3e8da6cda3794c0/pages/6098222361/SPIKE+ML+Evaluation+Framework)

### 1.5 Deployment & Serving

- Packaging models for inference (model registration, container building)
- Deploying to production infrastructure (SageMaker, TEAMServe/Triton, k8s)
- Managing canary/progressive rollouts with config-driven routing
- Post-deployment verification checks before serving production traffic [Model Deployments in ML Lifecycle](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6122166900/Model+Deployments+in+ML+Lifecycle)

### 1.6 Monitoring & Continuous Improvement

- Tracking model performance in production (latency, throughput, error rates)
- Detecting data drift and concept drift
- Triggering automated retraining pipelines when metrics degrade
- Running online A/B experiments (via Statsig) to validate improvements [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740/CoreML+MLOps+Enhancement+Plan+Improving+Quality+and+Observability+Across+Pipelines)

