## 4. Domain Operational Artifacts

These are the artifacts that **encode how ML work is actually performed** — the institutional knowledge embedded in templates, schemas, and configurations.

### 4.1 Evaluation Frameworks & Metric Definitions

**Internal evaluation tools:**
- **rovo-eval (Assistant Evaluation Service)**: Web UI for scheduling evaluations, multi-turn conversation simulation, golden dataset validation, SQS-based async evaluation
- **AIX-eval**: ML Studio-native evaluation, Python-based custom metrics, prompt optimization workflows, production data access
- **ML Lens**: Centralized platform for tracking model metrics across the lifecycle (data quality → training → serving)

**Metric categories tracked:**
- Classification: precision, recall, F1-score, AUC-ROC
- Ranking: MRR, NDCG@K
- Regression: RMSE, MAE, R²,sMAPE
- LLM-specific: LLM-as-a-judge scores (accuracy, helpfulness, safety), hallucination detection
- Business: CTR, conversion rates, revenue lift

Source: [SPIKE: ML Evaluation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202036af61f960604897b3e8da6cda3794c0/pages/6098222361/SPIKE+ML+Evaluation+Framework)

### 4.2 Model Cards & Responsible AI Documentation

Model cards at Atlassian document:
- **Solution overview and intended use** — what the model does, target population
- **Training data** — sources, licenses, demographic composition
- **Relevant factors** — known limitations, out-of-distribution behavior
- **Testing and validation** — performance metrics sliced by group (region, cohort)
- **Responsible AI considerations** — fairness metrics, bias mitigation approaches, error balance across groups
- **Human-in-the-loop processes** — where human oversight is required

**Fairness checklist (from AI fairness documentation):**
- ☐ Fairness goal chosen and documented
- ☐ Metrics sliced by group (incl. false-pos/neg)
- ☐ Bias mitigation attempted and recorded
- ☐ Data sources, rights, retention documented
- ☐ Access controls + audit logs enabled
- ☐ Model card + rollback plan in place

Source: [AI fairness & data risks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121eeecde7086e94a778d189b8b5d0a90f2/pages/5749449742/AI+fairness+data+risks), [CVS Enterprise AI Governance assessment](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/TRUSTHELP-6073)

### 4.3 Data Quality Validation Schemas

The **Quality SDK** provides composable, extensible data quality checks:

```python
@dataclass
class CheckResult:
    name: str        # e.g. "snapshot_exists"
    passed: bool
    metric: Dict[str, Any]
    message: str = ""
```

**Standard quality checks implemented:**
- Zero-Value Check (column population)
- Freshness Check (data recency)
- Volume/Day-over-Day change detection
- Schema validation (input data meets expected format)
- Distribution shift detection (PSI, KS test, Wasserstein distance, Chi-squared, JSD)
- Null rate monitoring
- Join key cardinality validation

**Drift detection framework:**
| Feature Type | Test | Threshold |
|---|---|---|
| Continuous | Population Stability Index (PSI) | <0.1 no drift,0.1–0.25 moderate, >0.25 significant |
| Continuous | Kolmogorov-Smirnov Test | p-value < 0.05 = significant |
| Categorical | Chi-squared Test | p-value < 0.05 |
| Categorical | Jensen-Shannon Divergence | 0 = identical, 1 = maximally different |

Source: [Quality SDK: Architecture & Check Reference](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6520868784/Quality+SDK+Architecture+Check+Reference), [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740/CoreML+MLOps+Enhancement+Plan+Improving+Quality+and+Observability+Across+Pipelines)

### 4.4 A/B Test Analysis Templates

Standard A/B test documentation includes:
- **Objective**: Testing goal + hypothesis
- **KPIs**: Primary metric, secondary metrics, guardrail (do-no-harm) metrics
- **Targeting rule**: Targeting level (instance or user), targeting logic
- **Cohorting data**: Experiment name, Test/Control IDs, exposed event name, exposing rule
- **Sample sizing**: Test/control instance counts, user counts, exposed users
- **Analysis**: Pre-analysis link, test result link, result communication link
- **Go-live checklist**: Product Management☐, Analytics ☐, Engineering ☐

Source: [(TEMPLATE) A/B Test Summary](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/cds3/pages/509994701/TEMPLATE+A+B+Test+Summary), [A/B Testing Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DEx/pages/6600572710/A+B+Testing+Runbook)

### 4.5 Training Pipeline DAGs (ML Studio Workflows)

ML Studio workflows are defined as DAGs of modules:
1. **Data Preparation Module** — reads from data sources, computes features, outputs training sets
2. **Training Module** — runs training loops with MLflow instrumentation
3. **Evaluation Module** — computes metrics, writes to ML Lens
4. **Packaging Module** — produces model artifacts in standard format
5. **Registration Module** — registers model in ML Registry (Tarot V3)

**Module descriptor YAML** specifies:
- `consumes`: fully qualified table names for inputs
- `produces`: output tables/artifacts
- `parameters`: configurable hyperparameters
- `compute`: resource requirements (GPU type, memory)

Source: [ML Studio: Workflow Authorship & Execution](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3300965181/ML+Studio+Workflow+Authorship+Execution)

### 4.6 Inference Optimization Playbooks

Documented optimization techniques organized by level:

**Hardware-level**: ASIC vs CPU vs GPU selection, cost/performance tradeoffs
**Software-level**: CUDA kernels, DNN libraries, DL compilers
**Model post-training**: Pruning, quantization (INT8, FP16), knowledge distillation
**Request-level**: Dynamic batching, chunking, optimal batch size selection
**Network-level**: Protocol optimization, request caching, semantic caching, KV caching for LLMs
**Training-time suggestions**: Architecture choices that enable faster inference

Source: [CAF MLSE Design Interview - Inference ML Infra](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020ff70b14e5dca488b8ea018f735980b62/pages/5151531820/CAF+MLSE+Design+Interview+-+Inference+ML+Infra)

### 4.7 ML CI/CD Pipeline Configurations

A three-stage CI/CD framework:

**Stage 1 — On Every PR/Push:**
- Lint & type check, unit tests, vulnerability scan, data schema validation

**Stage 2 — On Merge to Main:**
- Train model → evaluate → quality gate → register in MLflow → deploy to staging → integration tests → deploy to production (with automatic rollback)

**Stage 3 — Scheduled (Daily/Weekly):**
- Drift monitoring → auto-retrain trigger → performance report publication

Source: [ML Pipeline CI/CD Validation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6723101866/ML+Pipeline+CI+CD+Validation+Framework+core-ml-batch-pipelines)

---

## 5. The MLE's Relationship with Data

### 5.1 Data Pipeline Ownership Boundaries

The MLE's data ownership model operates at multiple levels:

| Boundary | MLE Owns | Data Engineering Owns |
|---|---|---|
| **Feature Engineering** | Feature definitions, transformations, quality checks | Raw data pipelines, data lake infrastructure |
| **Training Data** | Dataset curation, labeling strategy, train/val/test splits | Data governance compliance enforcement |
| **Feature Store** | Feature view definitions (DSL), feature serving configuration | Online/offline store infrastructure, materialization engine |
| **Production Data** | Feature consumption patterns, model input validation | Pipeline orchestration, data freshness SLAs |

### 5.2 Data Quality Gates

Atlassian implements quality gates at multiple pipeline stages:

1. **Feature ingestion**: Schema validation, null rate checks, zero-value checks
2. **Training data preparation**: Distribution analysis, outlier detection, freshness verification
3. **Training-serving skew detection**: Same transformation logic for online/offline
4. **Production inference**: Input validation, output anomaly detection
5. **Post-deployment**: Continuous drift monitoring (data drift + concept drift)

Source: [ML Data - Data Quality (Atlas project)](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-101802)

### 5.3 Feature Engineering Patterns

Typical feature engineering flows at Atlassian:

```python
# MLFS Batch Feature View DSL (Atlassian's feature definition as code)
# Defines: data source → transformation → materialization → online/offline serving
atlassian_batch_event_track = BatchSource(
    name="atlassian_batch_event_track",
    batch_config=track_events_combined_data_source
)
```

**Patterns documented:**
- Behavioral features: Click counts, view sequences, interaction patterns (aggregated from Plato/Kafka events via Databricks)
- Temporal aggregations: 7-day, 30-day rolling windows
- Cross-entity joins: User×Tenant×Document features
- Real-time features: Stream sources with batch backfill for point-in-time correctness

### 5.4 Data Versioning & Lineage Tracking

**Dreamcatcher ML Metadata Domain Model** tracks:
- `ML Workflow` → produces → `ML Model`
- `ML Workflow` → consumes → `ML Dataset`
- `ML Deployment` → contains → `ML Model`
- `ML Model` → has → `ML Metric`

Lineage is **automatically captured** when using ML Studio and ML Registry V3. Tables must be specified in `consumes`/`produces` fields with fully qualified names for lineage to be traced.

Source: [Dreamcatcher: ML Metadata Domain Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5552928221/Dreamcatcher+ML+Metadata+Domain+Model)

---

## 6. Model Deployment & Serving Patterns

### 6.1 Deployment Providers

Atlassian maintains three model deployment providers:

| Provider | Use Case | Characteristics |
|---|---|---|
| **TEAMServe** (Triton/Kubernetes) | High-performance GPU inference, multi-model | Quantization, LoRA, batch + real-time, KEDA autoscaling |
| **MSP / SageMaker** | Standard model serving | Real-time (60s timeout,6MB payload) + Async (1GB payload via SQS) |
| **Launchpad** | Testing and validation | Performance profiling before production promotion |

### 6.2 Serving Patterns

| Pattern | Description | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Online query inference** | Real-time, latency-sensitive predictions | Search query embeddings, content classification |
| **Real-time indexing inference** | Event-driven embedding generation via async queues | Document update → embedding → index update |
| **Offline batch inference** | Periodic bulk processing on scalable ML infrastructure | Training set generation, bulk scoring, recommendation pre-computation |

Source: [Serve Search Embedding models in TEAMServe](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202061551fe70fbc403cbc67930b9b879754/pages/5848526238/Serve+Search+Embedding+models+in+TEAMServe)

### 6.3 Canary & Progressive Rollout

The Model Service entity supports:
- **Active deployment config**: shadow, canary, progressive rollout
- **Config-driven routing**: Environment-gated (dev/staging/prod) and percent-gated (1% → 5% → 25% → 100%)
- **A/B Testing via Statsig**: Feature gates controlling traffic allocation to model versions
- **Model hot-swapping**: Changing model version behind an existing endpoint without endpoint recreation

### 6.4 Shadow Scoring

- Deploy new model alongside production model
- Run shadow test after deployment (endpoint + Searcher update)
- Compare shadow predictions against production without affecting users
- Use Launchpad for model validation in shadow mode

Source: [Model Deployment Agility - Requirements](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CentAI/pages/6081033354/Model+Deployment+Agility+-+Requirements)

### 6.5 Rollback Procedures

- **Post-Deployment Verification**: Evaluation dataset runs must pass before production traffic is served
- **Safe-rollback wrapper script**: Preserves ASG floor during rollbacks
- **Model version staging**: Archive old Production versions, transition new version to Production; only ONE version in Production stage
- **Automatic rollback triggers**: Performance metrics (latency, error rates) exceeding thresholds
- **Config-based rollback**: Revert deployment config to previous model version

### 6.6 Model Compression & Optimization

Documented patterns (ML Fabric Recipes Guide):
- **Quantization**: INT8/FP16 for inference acceleration
- **Pruning**: Removing redundant weights post-training
- **LoRA**: Low-Rank Adaptation for efficient fine-tuning
- **Distillation**: Training smaller models from larger teacher models
- **Triton ensemble inference**: Pipeline multiple models for combined inference

Source: [ML Orchestration Roadmap](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202091e93be306a64a9b8d5e1faf780e8820/pages/7013482971/ML+Orchestration+Roadmap+From+Databricks+to+ML+Fabric+on+Kubernetes)

---

## 7. The MLE User Journey — Synthesized Lifecycle

Drawing from Atlassian's MLP feedback and MLE lifecycle documentation:

### Phase 1: Experimentation
- Access data via ML Studio/Labs notebooks
- Build features using MLFS/Tecton/Plato+Socrates
- Run offline evaluations (ML Studio notebooks, MLflow, ML Lens)
- Tune training parameters, track in MLflow

### Phase 2: Development
- Real data access with compliance (DG and Privacy approval)
- Model development in ML Studio (DS and MLEs)
- Model training on ML Fabric compute (GPU clusters)
- Model choice via AI Gateway (cost/performance optimization)
- Serving choice: TEAMServe or MSP

### Phase 3: Release/Serving
- Model registration in ML Registry
- Deployment to TEAMServe/MSP endpoints
- A/B experiment execution via Statsig
- Production monitoring via ML Lens + observability stack
- Drift detection and automated retraining triggers

Source: [MLP feedback from MLEs & Focus areas for FY27](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63466da3cafc998a91cf5634/pages/7045633174/MLP+feedback+from+MLEs+Focus+areas+for+FY27)

---

## 8. Key Operational Patterns & Institutional Knowledge

### 8.1 The "80/20 Rule" Philosophy
Across all MLE JDs at Atlassian: "Focus on business practicality and the 80/20 rule; very high bar for output quality, but recognize the business benefit of 'having something now' vs 'perfection sometime in the future'." This is deeply embedded in MLE culture — velocity between conceptualization and initial output is prized.

### 8.2 Applied ML Distribution
The talent landscape shows MLE role distribution: **Applied ML (67%)**, Generalist (19%), Research (11%), Infrastructure (3%). The vast majority of MLE work is applied — shipping production systems, not publishing papers.

### 8.3 Critical Skill Gap: ML Design
90% of final panel interview failures for MLEs are in **ML Design** — the ability to design end-to-end systems given ambiguous requirements. This signals that system-level thinking (not just model building) is the highest-value differentiator.

Source: [Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer (MLE)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE)

---

## 9. Summary: Grounding the Role in Technical Reality

The MLE role is fundamentally an **engineering role with ML specialization** — not a research role that occasionally deploys. The concrete technical reality shows:

1. **Daily work is dominated by data and engineering** — exploring data, building features, debugging pipelines, optimizing latency — not algorithmic innovation
2. **Toolchain is enterprise-grade** — MLflow + Kubernetes + feature stores + model registries + CI/CD, not Jupyter notebooks and ad-hoc scripts
3. **Operational artifacts encode institutional knowledge** — evaluation harnesses, quality SDKs, A/B templates, deployment configs, lineage graphs are the true "source of truth" for how work gets done
4. **Full-cycle ownership is the differentiator** — problem framing through production monitoring, including on-call responsibility for model health
5. **The platform is the multiplier** — MLEs don't build infrastructure from scratch; they use ML Studio, ML Fabric, MLFS, TEAMServe, ML Lens as an integrated platform that handles orchestration, compute, serving, and monitoring

The operational artifacts (DSL feature definitions, module descriptor YAMLs, Quality SDK check configurations, deployment pipeline configs, MLflow experiment structures, A/B test templates, model cards) collectively form the **executable specification** of how ML work is performed — they are as important to understanding the role as any algorithm textbook.[0m
