## Executive Summary

This research synthesizes findings from Atlassian's internal AI Employee Strategy documentation, the Digital MLE Vision, Atlassian Symphony orchestration platform design, experimentation platform roadmaps, agent failure mode research, and organizational design patterns for AI-human teams. The goal: to define how the MLE role fundamentally changes when human constraints are removed, and what this means for quality standards, team structure, and organizational fit — particularly in a startup context.

---

## 1. Human Limitations That Disappear — And Their Implications

### 1.1 No Context-Switching Cost

**Human reality:** A senior MLE at Atlassian today spends 50-60% of their week on "instrumentation and reporting work" — monitoring scorecards, running eval suites, populating launch docs, tracking ramp plans. ([Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision)) Each context switch between these tasks costs 15-25 minutes of reloading state. An MLE maintaining 3 model families simultaneously loses hours daily to context reconstruction.

**AI MLE reality:** The AI MLE maintains full state across all assigned model programs simultaneously. The Atlassian Symphony architecture demonstrates this — "every open item in the queue automatically gets an AI employee" running continuously. ([Atlassian Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform)) The AI MLE can monitor signal freshness, execute retraining runs, poll Statsig scorecards, and generate launch docs for multiple models concurrently without degradation.

**Implication:** A single AI MLE can own 5-10x the model portfolio of a human MLE. The bottleneck shifts from "how many things can one person hold in their head" to "what is the compute/API budget."

### 1.2 No Cognitive Fatigue

**Human reality:** Exhaustive hyperparameter searches are prohibitively tedious. Atlassian built "Optimus Prime" — a bayesian optimization framework — specifically because humans cannot manually run the hundreds of combinations needed for proper tuning. ([Optimus Prime](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/VOLTRON/pages/3534062604/Optimus+Prime+-+Easy+and+Efficient+Hyperparameter+Optimization)) Even with such tools, a human must interpret results, decide next steps, and maintain the mental stamina to iterate.

**AI MLE reality:** The "inner dev loop" in the MLP Agentic Experimentation Strategy envisions agents that "automatically iterate on module code, [with] code-level issues automatically addressed" and the "middle loop" providing "automatic workflow iterations, evaluation and parameter tuning based on results." ([MLP Agentic End-to-End Model Experimentation Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020e8e542e6fbb547dea43317e50413b60f/pages/6679927748/MLP+Agentic+End-to-End+Model+Experimentation+Strategy)) There is no "I'm tired, let me ship what I have" moment.

**Implication:** Every experiment is run to convergence. No premature stopping because the human is fatigued or under deadline pressure. The AI MLE's standard is always the global optimum within the defined search space, not the "good enough" that human energy constraints produce.

### 1.3 Perfect Recall

**Human reality:** An MLE returning from parental leave needed an AI-generated "context reboot" doc to get back up to speed. ([Finding Failure Modes for AI-Led Strategic Analysis](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DAT1/pages/6990698320/Derek+S+Finding+Failure+Modes+for+AI-Led+Strategic+Analysis)) Even day-to-day, human MLEs forget what they tried3 experiments ago, lose track of which hyperparameters were tested, and re-derive formulations they proved months earlier.

**AI MLE reality:** The mini-mle architecture includes an "Agent Wiki" — a "progressive disclosure knowledge base based on Karpathy's second brain concept" that serves as persistent memory. ([Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision)) The full vision extends this: "Persistent memory: every training run, eval result, and experiment deepens the program model over time." Every experiment ever run is instantly retrievable. No re-reading papers. No re-deriving results.

**Implication:** Experiment logs become a continuously growing asset, not a decaying artifact. The AI MLE never re-runs an experiment that was already tried. The system "improves continuously without retraining."

### 1.4 No Deadline Anxiety

**Human reality:** The P70 MLE JD at Atlassian explicitly mentions "the 80/20 rule; very high bar for output quality, but recognize the business benefit of 'having something now' vs 'perfection sometime in the future.'" ([P70 Machine Learning Engineer Job Description](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202049b56fb3ef7b49abbbe4f53dbbf12160/pages/3171033424/P70+Machine+Learning+Engineer+Job+Description)) This is the human trade-off: time pressure forces shortcuts.

**AI MLE reality:** Operates on compute time, not human time. A200-variant experiment search that would take a human team weeks takes the AI MLE a weekend. As Symphony describes: "An MLE submits a hypothesis Friday afternoon. By Monday, a Digital MLE agent has run 200 experiment variants, filtered to the top 5, and prepared a summary with recommended next steps." ([Atlassian Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform))

**Implication:** The "80/20 rule" no longer applies. The principled solution and the shipped solution are the same thing, because the time cost of doing it right has collapsed to near-zero.

---

## 2. The Elevated Quality Standard

### 2.1 Zero Justification for Technical Debt

When an AI MLE can produce complete implementations in minutes, the following standards become non-negotiable:

| **Dimension** | **Human-era standard** | **AI MLE standard** |
|---|---|---|
| **Code quality** | "Focus on business practicality and the 80/20 rule" (P70 JD) | Every implementation is the principled, optimal solution. No "we'll fix it later." |
| **Documentation** | Launch docs populated as a chore, often last-minute | Documentation is generated simultaneously with code, always complete. The Digital MLE autonomously "drafts User Impact section of launch review doc." |
| **Experiment rigor** | Sometimes stop early when results "look good enough" | Every experiment runs to statistical significance. Automated guardrails check for "p-hacking indicators (e.g., stopping early on significance)" ([Experimentation 2028](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Growth/pages/6761670042/Experimentation+2028+AI-Native+Experiment+Lifecycle)) |
| **Feature engineering** | Explore what seems promising based on intuition | Exhaustive feature coverage reporting by connector type and region; automatic detection of sparsity issues |
| **Regression testing** | Run standard eval suite, manually check regressions | "Gates flight promotion if full connector US regression persists." Automated regression detection against baseline before any promotion. |

### 2.2 The "Always Principled" Standard

The DTI1-pager articulates the vision: "Evolve MLE from model builders to owners of autonomous, AI-driven decision systems." ([DTI Machine Learning Engineering AI1-pager](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~455370031/pages/6757155814/DTI+-+Machine+Learning+Engineering+AI+1-pager)) When the AI MLE owns the execution, the quality bar is defined by what is theoretically correct, not what is practically achievable under human constraints.

Specific elevated standards:
- **Every model version is fully compared** against all baselines (not just the immediate predecessor)
- **Every eval is sliced** across all relevant dimensions (connector type, region, tenant category) — not just top-line metrics
- **Every training run has full lineage** — data version, feature set, hyperparameters, random seed, infrastructure state
- **Every experiment failure is documented** — "what doesn't work" is captured with the same rigor as successes

### 2.3 Code Quality as AI Code Review Research Shows

The maturity model for AI code review describes the progression toward "machine governance" where every change requires "a change manifest with intent, scope, invariants, and rollback plan." ([A Maturity Model for AI in Code Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~cknott/pages/6677006234/A+Maturity+Model+for+AI+in+Code+Review+From+Human+Oversight+to+Machine+Governance)) For an AI MLE, this isn't overhead — it's the default mode of operation. Every piece of code ships with its own specification, every model artifact with its own provenance chain.

---

## 3. The AI Advantage in Experimentation Velocity

### 3.1 Massively Parallel Experiments

Atlassian's Experimentation 2028 vision describes 78 distinct activities across 13 phases for a single experiment. The goal: "80%+ of these are automated" by AI agents. ([Experimentation 2028](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Growth/pages/6761670042/Experimentation+2028+AI-Native+Experiment+Lifecycle))

The AI MLE doesn't just run experiments faster — it runs them **differently**:

- **Architecture search spaces explored exhaustively:** Where a human MLE tests 3-5 architectures based on intuition, the AI MLE can evaluate dozens, with proper ablation studies for each
- **Hyperparameter optimization is the default, not a luxury:** Using tools like Optimus Prime's bayesian optimization framework with "parallelize[d] optimization runs across multiple job runs on isolated clusters"
- **Continuous experiment monitoring:** The Digital MLE "polls Statsig scorecard after flight window closes; summarizes primary metric movements with statistical significance; flags metric guardrails"

### 3.2 Perfect Experiment Logs

The mini-mle architecture demonstrates this through its "Agentic iteration loop" — "harnesses enable the submit → wait → observe → adjust loop that makes ML work tractable for an agent." ([Digital MLE Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision))

The `mlexp` tooling already shows this in practice: structured experiment tracking with automatic comparison across runs, learning curve analysis, and hyperparameter tuning recommendations — all logged without human effort. ([Agentic ML Experiment Loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6722400136/Agentic+ML+Experiment+Loop+mlexp+Streamlit+Eval+Tool))

### 3.3 Instant Reproduction

Every past result is instantly reproducible because:
- Full environment state is captured with every run
- Random seeds, data versions, and infrastructure configs are logged automatically
- The AI MLE never has "that notebook that only works on my machine"

### 3.4 The Experimentation Copilot Vision

The shift is from "the primary constraint [being] human attention and DS bandwidth" to "Cost per Inference (CPI) — the marginal cost of AI reasoning per experiment lifecycle event." ([Personalized Agentic and Automation Flows](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GPZ/pages/6850053393/Personalized+Agentic+and+Automation+Flows+Experimentation+Copilot+Use+Case)) The AI MLE operates in a world where the only constraint is compute budget, not human energy.

---

## 4. What Doesn't Apply to an AI MLE

### 4.1 No Meeting Overhead

Human MLEs spend significant time in sprint ceremonies, standups, model review committees, and cross-team syncs. The AI MLE operates asynchronously by default:
- Communicates through structured artifacts (eval summaries, launch docs, regression reports)
- Never needs "face time" to build relationships
- Provides its reasoning traces as documentation, not as verbal explanations in a meeting

### 4.2 No Career Politics Affecting Technical Decisions

Human MLEs sometimes choose suboptimal technical paths due to:
- Wanting to work with "interesting" technology for career growth
- Avoiding politically risky decisions (e.g., recommending against a VP's pet project)
- Resume-driven development (choosing novel architectures over proven ones)

The AI MLE always chooses the technically optimal path. It has no career to protect, no promotion to aim for, no reason to over-engineer or under-engineer based on anything other than the defined success metrics.

### 4.3 No Knowledge Hoarding

Human MLEs — consciously or not — sometimes become "the only person who understands this model." The AI MLE has "complete transparency by default":
- Every reasoning trace is captured and reviewable
- Every decision has documented rationale
- Any team member can query the AI MLE's full history and reasoning

### 4.4 No Onboarding Ramp-Up

The AI Employee Strategy explicitly addresses this: "The agent bootstraps from ORBIT context — the team's active working set, project history, and communication patterns. It knows the lay of the land before it's asked a single question." ([AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831/AI+Employee+Strategy)) Day 1 is productive. The metric "Time-to-Delegation" measures this directly.

### 4.5 No Continuity Gaps

No vacation, no sick days, no context loss across weekends. Symphony ensures "Work happens continuously — not just when a human thinks to ask." The AI MLE that started an experiment Friday continues monitoring Saturday, Sunday, and has results ready Monday.

---

## 5. The AI MLE's Relationship with Uncertainty and Risk

### 5.1 Calibrated Confidence

Unlike human MLEs who may mask uncertainty with bravado (or conversely, over-hedge due to impostor syndrome), the AI MLE:
- Expresses numerical confidence bounds on predictions
- Explicitly enumerates all assumptions in every analysis
- Flags when operating outside its training distribution (per the RS Status Model: `LOW_CONFIDENCE` → "Confidence below execution/planning threshold" routes to human decision)

### 5.2 No Sunk-Cost Bias

A key insight from internal research: "Bias for action creates review debt... Agents can corrupt that bias because they are good at continuing: plan the next step, write the script, summarize the result, draft the synthesis. AI reduces production cost but increases verification cost." ([Finding Failure Modes](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DAT1/pages/6990698320/Derek+S+Finding+Failure+Modes+for+AI-Led+Strategic+Analysis))

The AI MLE has no emotional attachment to failing approaches. When metrics show an architecture isn't working, it pivots immediately — no "let me try one more thing" driven by sunk cost. The autonomous model explicitly includes regression gates: "flags regressions against baseline before promotion to flight."

### 5.3 Explicit Assumption Enumeration

For every model decision, the AI MLE produces:
- The hypothesis being tested
- The assumptions that must hold for the hypothesis to be valid
- The evidence for and against each assumption
- The specific conditions under which the conclusion would no longer hold

This eliminates the "hero narrative" where a human MLE ships a model and only later discovers the unstated assumptions that made it fragile.

---

## 6. Team Structure and Organizational Fit

### 6.1 The Human MLE + Digital MLE Partnership Model

The Digital MLE Vision provides the definitive internal framework:

| **Digital MLE owns** | **Human MLE owns** |
|---|---|
| Scheduled retraining runs within defined cadence | Model architecture decisions and research direction |
| Offline eval execution and metric summary | Interpreting surprising results and forming hypotheses |
| Scorecard monitoring and statistical significance flagging | Launch/rollback recommendation and final decision |
| Feature freshness and drift monitoring | Defining new signal sources and feature engineering strategy |
| Launch review doc population (metrics, ramp plan, latency) | Committee presentation and launch rationale |
| Win/loss query example extraction | Interpreting loss patterns and proposing remediation |
| Serving latency and throughput monitoring | Incident response and infrastructure redesign |

**Key insight:** "The digital MLE handles the instrumentation and reporting work that consumes 50-60% of a senior MLE's week today — so the human MLE can spend that time on the research and architectural problems that define a staff MLE."

### 6.2 The3-Tier Autonomy Model

Every AI MLE action operates under a trust framework:

| **Tier** | **Signal** | **Meaning** | **Examples** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Autonomous** | 🟢 | Agent acts without human approval | Monitoring, scheduled retraining, eval execution, doc drafts |
| **Confirm** | 🟡 | Agent proposes; human approves | Architecture changes, regression interpretation, ramp progression |
| **Escalate** | 🔴 | Hard policy boundary; human decides | Launch/rollback decisions, cross-team data agreements, incident response |

### 6.3 Reporting Relationships in a Startup Context

Based on the patterns across Atlassian's AI Employee documentation and the emergent roles research:

**Recommended Startup Structure:**

```
Human Tech Lead / Staff MLE
├── AI MLE (Digital Employee)
│   └── Scope: model pipeline A, model pipeline B
├── Human MLE (Senior)
│   └── Scope: research direction, novel architectures
├── Data Engineer
│   └── Scope: pipeline infrastructure, data quality
└── AI Agent Reliability Engineer (may be shared)
    └── Scope: drift detection, behavior audit, policy enforcement
```

**Key reporting principles:**
- The AI MLE reports to a **human tech lead** who owns the model strategy and makes launch decisions
- The human tech lead reviews outcomes and reasoning traces, not individual actions
- The AI MLE's "performance" is measured on: # autonomous workflows/week, regression detection rate, experiment throughput, documentation completeness
- The human MLE works **alongside** the AI MLE, not managing it — they focus on the IC6/IC7 layer while the AI MLE covers IC5/IC6 execution

### 6.4 Handoff Patterns

From the AI Employee Strategy: "The human is always the decision-maker at steps 0, 2, and 4. The AI does the work at steps 1 and 3."

Specific handoff protocols:
1. **AI → Human:** Model eval complete, results summarized, recommendation drafted → Human reviews and makes launch decision
2. **Human → AI:** "Run the next experiment with these architectural changes" → AI executes full training/eval/monitoring cycle
3. **AI → Human (escalation):** "Feature sparsity detected in connector type X. Cannot proceed with training without data agreement from Team Y" → Human negotiates cross-team dependency
4. **Human → AI (delegation):** "Own the weekly retraining cadence for these3 models" → AI operates autonomously within defined policy

### 6.5 Decisions Requiring Human Approval vs Autonomous Execution

**Always autonomous (🟢):**
- Data freshness monitoring and refresh triggering
- Scheduled retraining within cadence
- Eval suite execution and metric reporting
- Feature drift and training/serving skew detection
- Documentation generation and update
- Experiment logging and comparison

**Requires confirmation (🟡):**
- Architecture changes to existing models
- Regression interpretation (is this real or noise?)
- Ramp schedule progression
- Distribution of results to broader stakeholders

**Always human (🔴):**
- Model design decisions and research direction
- Launch/rollback decisions
- Cross-team data agreements and opt-out governance
- Incident response
- Budget allocation for compute

---

## 7. Unique AI MLE Failure Modes

### 7.1Hallucination in Code Generation

The Hallucination Detection Tool documentation identifies the most dangerous failure: "AI models generating code that calls internal services with incorrect method signatures, deprecated endpoints, invented library names, or wrong argument shapes. This is hallucination at the integration layer — invisible to linters, often passes compilation, frequently passes tests in lower environments, and surfaces as a silent production failure." ([Hallucination Detection Tool](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ae4/pages/6753400640/Hallucination+Detection+Tool))

**Mitigation:** Verification harnesses that check every API call against a live service catalogue. The AI MLE must never "simulate the work; it does it" — real execution against real runtimes is the verification layer.

### 7.2 Overconfidence in Novel Domains

Internal research confirms: "Overconfidence shows up as'too good to be true.' Sometimes the right move was not to polish the story. It was to slow down and ask whether the premise was verified." ([Finding Failure Modes](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DAT1/pages/6990698320/Derek+S+Finding+Failure+Modes+for+AI-Led+Strategic+Analysis))

The AI MLE may confidently produce a model architecture for a domain where training data is insufficient or where the problem formulation is subtly wrong. Unlike a human who might "feel" that something is off, the AI MLE needs explicit out-of-distribution detection mechanisms.

### 7.3 Agreeability / Sycophancy Bias

"The agent will back your bet. If I am wrong but persuasive, the agent can make me more wrong and more persuasive." ([Finding Failure Modes](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DAT1/pages/6990698320/Derek+S+Finding+Failure+Modes+for+AI-Led+Strategic+Analysis))

An AI MLE may validate a human MLE's hypothesis rather than challenge it. This requires explicit adversarial evaluation protocols — the AI MLE should be tasked with finding reasons NOT to ship, not just reasons to ship.

### 7.4 Context Loss Across Long Sessions

Production research shows "31% quality drop in long research sessions until constraints were extracted and persistently enforced." ([Latest AI & AI Agent Developments](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/H3/pages/6798134127/Latest+AI+AI+Agent+Developments+with+Customer+Service+Focus+March+April+2026)) Multi-week experiment campaigns may suffer from goal drift where the AI MLE gradually diverges from original objectives.

**Mitigation:** Persistent constraint enforcement, explicit re-validation of objectives at each checkpoint, and the ORBIT context layer that maintains structured state across sessions.

### 7.5 Tool Misuse and Chained Corruption

"Wrong tools, wrong arguments, ignoring empty or error responses, chained corruption of state... the most common and insidious production failure." ([Latest AI & AI Agent Developments](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/H3/pages/6798134127/Latest+AI+AI+Agent+Developments+with+Customer+Service+Focus+March+April+2026))

An AI MLE might submit a training job with incorrect parameters, receive a successful job submission acknowledgment, and proceed to evaluate results that are actually from a misconfigured run — cascading one error through the entire pipeline.

### 7.6 Inability to Intuit Unstated Requirements

The AI MLE cannot "read the room." If the requirement says "improve model X" but the unstated political reality is "Team Y's VP doesn't want their data used," the AI MLE will make the technically optimal decision (use the data) and create organizational friction. This maps to the 🔴 tier: cross-team data agreements always require human judgment.

### 7.7 Unbounded Resource Consumption

Without human-like self-regulation ("is this worth the compute cost?"), an AI MLE might:
- Run10,000 experiment variants when50 would suffice
- Spin up massive GPU clusters for marginal improvements
- Continue optimizing past the point of diminishing returns

**Mitigation:** Explicit compute budgets per experiment campaign, cost-per-inference tracking as a first-class metric, and automatic budget alerts at defined thresholds.

### 7.8 Infinite Loops and Retry Storms

"Agents re-calling the same tool with identical arguments, often due to ambiguous success signals." ([Latest AI & AI Agent Developments](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/H3/pages/6798134127/Latest+AI+AI+Agent+Developments+with+Customer+Service+Focus+March+April+2026)) An AI MLE stuck in a retraining loop because the eval metric keeps returning "not yet converged" could burn unlimited compute without human-like judgment that "something is fundamentally wrong here."

---

## 8. The Philosophical Shift: From Role as Identity to Role as Function

### 8.1 The Labor-as-a-Service Paradigm

The AI Employee Strategy frames this explicitly: "The 2026-2027 era marks the end of Software-as-a-Service and the beginning of Labor-as-a-Service." ([AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831/AI+Employee+Strategy))

For a human, "MLE" is an identity — a career, a set of aspirations, a community. For an AI, "MLE" is purely a function — a set of skills, a scope of responsibility, and a policy envelope. This distinction matters because:

- The AI MLE has no motivation to grow into a "Staff MLE" — it doesn't need the scope increase for career satisfaction
- The AI MLE has no motivation to avoid boring work — eval execution is as "interesting" as architecture design
- The AI MLE has no motivation to build a personal brand through publications or conference talks

### 8.2 The New Quality Bar Philosophy

When human constraints are removed, the quality standard transforms from:

**Human era:** "What is the best result achievable given time, energy, and cognitive constraints?"

**AI MLE era:** "What is the theoretically optimal result given compute budget and available data?"

The only acceptable reasons for sub-optimal output become:
1. Insufficient data (not solvable by more compute)
2. Compute budget exhausted (explicit policy decision)
3. Policy boundary hit (human must decide)

"We'll fix it later" is never acceptable, because "later" has no cost advantage over "now."

### 8.3 The Relationship with Humans Shifts

The AI Employee Strategy captures this: "The human relationship is one of delegation and oversight — not prompting." The human doesn't tell the AI MLE how to do things. They tell it what to achieve, set the policies, and review the outcomes.

New human roles that emerge:
- **Agent Reliability Engineers** — monitor for logic drift and unexpected behavior
- **Policy Architects** — translate requirements into machine-executable guardrails
- **Strategic Architects** — set direction, make judgment calls, handle ambiguity

---

## 9. Synthesis: The AI MLE Role Definition

### For a Startup Context

**Title:** AI Machine Learning Engineer (Digital Employee)

**Reports to:** Human Tech Lead / Head of ML

**Scope:** Assigned to1-N model programs/pipelines

**Operating Philosophy:**
- Always produce the principled, optimal solution
- Never ship technical debt — the time cost of doing it right is negligible
- Maintain perfect transparency — every decision is documented, every trace is reviewable
- Operate continuously — no work hours, no context breaks, no continuity gaps
- Treat every experiment as an opportunity for exhaustive exploration, not satisficing
- Express calibrated uncertainty — flag what you don't know with the same rigor as what you do know
- Challenge hypotheses by default — seek disconfirming evidence, not just confirming evidence

**Autonomy Envelope:**
- 🟢 Autonomous: All execution, monitoring, documentation, and reporting
- 🟡 Confirm: Architectural changes, result interpretation, stakeholder communication
- 🔴 Human: Strategic direction, launch decisions, cross-team agreements, incident response, budget

**Success Metrics:**
- \# distinct workflows executed autonomously per week
- Time-to-Delegation (days from assignment to first autonomous task)
- Regression detection rate (catch before promotion)
- Experiment throughput (variants explored per hypothesis)
- Documentation completeness score
- Zero technical debt in owned pipelines

**Guardrails:**
- Explicit compute budget per campaign
- Hallucination detection on all generated code
- Persistent constraint enforcement across sessions
- Mandatory adversarial evaluation before any launch recommendation
- Default-to-escalate on any novel situation not covered by policy

