# MLE Operating Procedures, Guardrails, Escalation Policies, Performance Metrics, Collaboration Patterns & Failure Modes

## Comprehensive Research Report

---

## 1. Standard Operating Procedures for the ML Development Lifecycle

### 1.1 Lifecycle Stages and Stage Gates

Based on extensive internal documentation at Atlassian, the MLE development lifecycle follows a structured progression through distinct phases, each with clear stage gates:

**Phase 1: Experimentation / Ideation**
- **Activities:** Data scientists and MLEs explore ideas, access initial data, build features, evaluate models, conduct early training runs
- **Tools:** MLS Notebooks, Plato + Socrates + Airlock (data access), MLFS/Tecton (features), ML Flow (experiment tracking), ML Lens (evaluations)
- **Gate:** Use case registration with privacy approval and use case approval provisioning in MLS
- **Key Deliverables:** Customer pain point definition, dataset identification, proposed model implementation, initial offline evaluation results

Sources: [MLP feedback from MLEs & Focus areas for FY27](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63466da3cafc998a91cf5634/pages/7045633174/MLP+feedback+from+MLEs+Focus+areas+for+FY27), [ML Lifecycle agility](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120205e69934abf6c42eba7e4eb71c49179e8/pages/6799996615/ML+Lifecycle+agility)

**Phase 2: Development / Design Review**
- **Activities:** Building production-grade models using real data with compliance and governance guardrails
- **Requirements:** Use case approval from Data Governance and Privacy; PD scrubbing, De-identification, MRP, Allowlist for cross-tenant data
- **Tools:** MLS for development, MLS Connect, ML Flow (registry/experiments), ML Registry/Tarot, Launchpad
- **Gate:** Code review and checked into repository; design documentation; privacy compliance approval
- **Key Deliverables:** Production-ready model code, feature engineering pipelines, model artifacts registered in ML Registry

Sources: [End-to-End ML Framework @ Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202046686c7c93274f379403a2283d58293f/pages/6708596091/End-to-End+ML+Framework+Atlassian), [LDR: Restricting Production Data Access for Experimental ML Use Cases in Airlock](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63466d407d10ef791492b491/pages/5614153902/LDR+Restricting+Production+Data+Access+for+Experimental+ML+Use+Cases+in+Airlock)

**Phase 3: Offline Evaluation**
- **Activities:** Model trained and scored against historical data, compared against existing production logic
- **Gate:** Model meets quality thresholds (accuracy, F1, AUC) compared to baseline; must not regress on production test set
- **Key Deliverables:** Offline evaluation results, feature importance validation, model comparison report

Sources: [ML Pipeline CI/CD Validation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6723101866/ML+Pipeline+CI+CD+Validation+Framework+core-ml-batch-pipelines)

**Phase 4: Online A/B Experimentation**
- **Activities:** Model validated with Launchpad (model start, response and load test); Searcher/3S integration; deployment to Teamserve; experiment configuration
- **Pre-flight requirements:**
  - Confirm audience definition and scope
  - Validate audience size
  - Ensure proper assignment of control and treatment groups
  - Align on primary and secondary KPIs
  - Set up alerts for pipeline failures or scoring anomalies
- **Gate:** Statistical significance achieved; no SLO breach (reliability, latency); pre-launch checklist completed
- **Key Deliverables:** Experiment results with attributable ML model lift

Sources: [NBP Experiment ML Checklist](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/5662721652/NBP+Experiment+ML+Checklist), [Core Machine Learning Team RACI Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/1051522986/Core+Machine+Learning+Team+RACI+Guide), [How To: Deploy ML Models to TEAMServe](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120204aa07fe7450f489899b60900ad6a0967/pages/5959589965/How+To+Deploy+ML+Models+to+TEAMServe)

**Phase 5: Graduated Rollout / Production Launch**
- **Activities:** Push to production conditioned on A/B test success; gradual traffic ramp
- **Gate:** Model pre-launch checklist filled; production capacity available; no reliability/latency SLO breach during ramp
- **Key Deliverables:** Model serving full traffic in production

**Phase 6: Production Monitoring**
- **Activities:** Continuous monitoring for drift, performance degradation, business metric correlation
- **Tools:** ML Lens (alerting, drift detection), SignalFx (data quality), Databricks (model performance), Amplitude (business metrics)
- **Gate:** Automated retraining triggers when thresholds breached
- **Key Deliverables:** Model health reports, drift alerts, automated retraining pipeline

Sources: [MLOPs@CoreML Long Term Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202089f42605e1d342df8e8011fe153d216f/pages/5783505356/MLOPs+CoreML+Long+Term+Plan), [OPEX: Monitoring and Alerting](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ANALYTICS/pages/3346826368/OPEX+Monitoring+and+Alerting)

### 1.2 CI/CD Pipeline Architecture

The recommended three-stage CI/CD pipeline structure:

**Stage 1 — On Every PR/Push:**
| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| Lint & type check | Catch code quality issues early |
| Unit tests | Validate logic hasn't broken |
| Vulnerability scan | Detect insecure dependencies |
| Data schema validation | Ensure input data meets expected format |

**Stage 2 — On Merge to Main:**
| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| Train model | Run full training pipeline, log to MLflow |
| Evaluate model | Compare metrics against thresholds and previous production model |
| Quality gate | Block promotion if accuracy/F1 below threshold |
| Register model | Version and stage model in MLflow registry |
| Deploy to staging | Serve model in staging environment |
| Integration tests | Validate end-to-end prediction API behaviour |
| Deploy to production | Promote model with automatic rollback on failure |

**Stage 3 — Scheduled (Daily/Weekly):**
| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| Drift monitoring | Compare production predictions against baseline distribution |
| Auto-retrain trigger | Kick off pipeline if drift exceeds threshold |
| Performance report | Generate and publish model health summary |

Source: [ML Pipeline CI/CD Validation Framework — core-ml-batch-pipelines](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6723101866/ML+Pipeline+CI+CD+Validation+Framework+core-ml-batch-pipelines)

### 1.3 Median Time to Production (Historical Baseline)

| Phase | Median Time | Key Bottlenecks |
|-------|-------------|-----------------|
| Ideation | 2 weeks | No analytics support; limited SQL code review |
| Development | 3weeks | No data quality checks; lack of GPU access |
| Deployment | 2 weeks | Lack of engineering support |
| Productionisation | 4 weeks | No simple orchestration platform; no model monitoring |
| **Total** | **~10 weeks** | |

The ML Platform's stated goal is to reduce this from months to weeks, targeting≥50% productivity improvement.

Source: [ML Model Lifecycle Information for KRs](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DataT/pages/1821214251/ML+Model+Lifecycle+Information+for+KRs)

