## 3. Escalation Policies

### 3.1 Incident Severity Levels for ML Systems

| Severity | Criteria | Example | Response |
|----------|----------|---------|----------|
| **SEV1– Critical** | Total service outage; significant customer data loss; complete model failure | Model serving 0predictions; complete pipeline failure | Immediate escalation; all hands on deck |
| **SEV2 – Major** | Significant degradation; partial outage >15 min | Model accuracy drops >20% from baseline; inference latency p99 exceeds SLO | Fix in next patch or hotfix |
| **SEV3 – Minor** | Total outage <5 min; noticeable performance degradation (usable) | Small latency increase; non-critical dashboard metrics not updating | Addressed in regular release cycles |

Source: [Runbook: MLFS Incident Priorities and Hotfix Process](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5749098244/Runbook+MLFS+Incident+Priorities+and+Hotfix+Process)

### 3.2 Alerting Thresholds and Escalation Triggers

**Drift/Degradation Alerting (ML Lens Best Practices):**

| Parameter | Production Phase | Experiment Phase |
|-----------|-----------------|------------------|
| Reference period | -2 weeks (medium-to-long) | -2 weeks |
| Tolerance | std≥ 3 (moderate/balanced) | std ≥ 4 (conservative for higher variance) |
| No-data threshold | 172800s (2 days, 2× expected ingestion) | Same |
| Alert fatigue reduction | N_OF threshold of 2 on drift alert | Same |

**Escalation trigger logic:**
```
If (semantic_drift_score > threshold) OR (action_trace_deviation > threshold)
 OR (data_deviation_percent > threshold)
 → Raise drift alert
```

Sources: [Per-Placement Alerting at Scale for KYC ML Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6967787922/Per-Placement+Alerting+at+Scale+for+KYC+ML+Observability), [Agent Reliability, Regression & Drift Detection Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AA7/pages/6097247892/Agent+Reliability+Regression+Drift+Detection+Framework+for+FOR+LLM-BASED+AGENTS)

### 3.3 Escalation Framework (Progressive Levels)

**Level 1: Awareness (Automated)**
- Trigger: Service appears in quarterly risk ranking above threshold
- Action: Auto-Slack notification to service owner with risk data, PIR links, recommended monitoring
- Cost: Zero marginal effort (scheduled query + Slack message)

**Level 2: Guided Self-Assessment**
- Trigger: Service remains high-risk for 2 consecutive quarters, OR is Tier 0/1 with80%+ undetected
- Action: Send Detection Health Checklist to service team
- Checklist includes: Availability monitoring, error rate alerting, latency alerting, data correctness validation, volume/completeness check, infrastructure resource alarms, dependency health probes, synthetic E2E tests

**Level 3: Incident-Informed Recommendation**
- Trigger: Service has a new Sev1/2incident where monitoring failed AND was already flagged at Level 1/2
- Action: Generate specific monitoring recommendations derived from the team's own PIR

**Level 4: Leadership Escalation**
- Trigger: Tier 0/1 service remains at80%+ undetected for 3+ quarters, OR same detection gap causes a repeat Sev1
- Action: Escalate to engineering director with full data trail

Source: [Proactive Detection Gap Identification & Escalation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120207e8021e18fdb4ef1ba128b9ade735fda/pages/7024210351/Proactive+Detection+Gap+Identification+Escalation+Framework)

### 3.4 Self-Remediation vs. Escalation Decision Matrix

**Self-remediate (automated or MLE-handled):**
- Automated retraining triggers based on: time-based (every 15 days), metric-based (accuracy below threshold), data drift (alert patterns shift significantly)
- Feature freshness alerts → retrigger materialization jobs
- Rate limiting / throttling → adjust batch size/concurrency
- Schema mismatch errors → fix and redeploy

**Escalate to ML Platform team:**
- ML Fabric infrastructure failures (GPU hardware faults, NCCL timeouts)
- Databricks Unity Catalog connection failures affecting multiple use cases
- Platform-wide throttling or quota issues

**Escalate to Data Engineering:**
- Upstream data pipeline failures
- Data schema changes breaking expectations
- Volume spikes/drops outside expected bounds

**Escalate to Product:**
- Model degradation affecting customer-facing KPIs beyond agreed thresholds
- A/B test results requiring business decision on launch/no-launch

Sources: [Alert Grouping 2.0 ML Deepdive](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203c51094a8b57411183fd83fdf08ef28c/pages/5928310239/Alert+Grouping+2.0+ML+Deepdive), [ML Fabric Capabilities and their Alerts](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6536338411/ML+Fabric+Capabilities+and+their+Alerts)

